  <div id="sutta">
  
    <h4 id="thag1.86" class="h1">Thag 1:86  Nāgita</h4>
            <div class="verse">
              <p>Outside of this path,</p>
              <p>the path of the many</p>
              <p>who teach other things</p>
              <p>doesn’t go to unbinding</p>
              <p class="v2">as does this:</p>
              <p>Thus the Blessed One</p>
              <p>instructs the Saṅgha,</p>
              <p>truly showing the palms of his hands.<span class="fn"><a href="#thag1.86note01">1</a></span><!--end fn cite--></p>
            </div><!--end verse-->
            <div class="note">
              <p class="notetitle">Note</p><!--end notetitle-->
              <p id="thag1.86note01">1. This is a reference to the fact that the Buddha was an “open-handed” teacher who did not wait to the end of his life to give his most crucial teachings. See <a href="../../DN/DN16.html">DN 16</a>. The message of this verse may be related to the fact that the Buddha addressed three of his most plain-speaking suttas to Nāgita: <a href="../../AN/AN5_30.html">AN 5:30</a>, <a href="../../AN/AN6_42.html">AN 6:42</a>, and <a href="../../AN/AN8_103.html">AN 8:103</a>.</p>
            </div><!--end notes-->
      </div><!--end:sutta-->
    </div><!--end:content-->
    
